ndence of observations.\n2. Normality of the response variable within each group.\n3. Homogeneity of variances across groups (homoscedasticity).\n\n**When to Use / Applicability / Selection Criteria:**\nANOVA is applicable when comparing means across multiple groups, particularly in experiments designed to evaluate the effects of multiple factors. It is selected when the researcher aims to understand not only the main effects of each factor but also their interactions.\n\n**Analysis Procedure:**\nThe analysis involves calculating the F-statistic, which is the ratio of the variance between the group means to the variance within the groups. If the F-statistic exceeds a critical value from the F-distribution, the null hypothesis (that all group means are equal) is rejected, indicating significant differences among the means.\n\n**Advantages and Strengths:**\n- ANOVA can handle multiple groups simultaneously, reducing the risk of Type I error compared to multiple t-tests.\n- It provides insights into interactions between factors, which can be crucial for understanding complex systems.\n- Factorial designs are efficient, allowing for the exploration of multiple factors with fewer experimental runs.\n\n**Limitations and Disadvantages:**\n- ANOVA is sensitive to violations of its assumptions, particularly normality and homogeneity of variances.\n- It does not indicate which specific groups are different; post-hoc tests are required for this purpose.\n- Factorial designs can become complex and resource-intensive as the number of factors and levels increases.\n\n**Related Designs, Methods, or Alternatives:**\nRelated methodologies include regression analysis, which can also assess the effects of multiple predictors, and response surface methodology, which is used for optimizing processes. Alternatives to factorial designs include completely randomized designs and block designs, which may be more suitable in certain contexts.\n\n**Practical Implementation Considerations:**\nW